Description: Cache temp*_input and fan*_input for 1 s (hwmon read cache)
 temp*_input and fan*_input are mode 0444. Every read took data->lock
 with mutex_lock() and did live EC/PMC2 I/O: one EC RAM read (command
 0x80) per temperature, up to 4 x 3 PMC2 reads per tachometer, within the
 1 s access budget of 0005. There was no cache and no minimum interval.
 A local user without privileges (also a container that sees the host's
 sysfs) could loop on these files from many threads: the pwm/pwm_enable
 writes of the regulator and the unbind restore queued behind every
 reader, and the EC got a continuous command stream (audit-20261016
 BACKLOG #28, c-review rc2 DOS-001; reviewer finding, not measured).
 .
 Now each of the 7 inputs (temp1-temp4, fan1-fan3) keeps the result of
 its last refresh (raw value or error code) with a jiffies timestamp and
 a valid flag in struct n5_data (usual hwmon valid/last_updated pattern).
 A read within N5_CACHE_MS = 1000 ms after the last cached refresh of
 that input is served from the cache under data->lock without port I/O.
 Otherwise the read does exactly the EC access it did before (same
 transaction, same n5_access_begin() budget) and stores the result. The
 timestamp is taken after the EC access, so two refreshes of one input
 start at least 1 s plus the access time apart.
 .
 Granularity: one input, not the whole group. A group refresh (all four
 temperatures or all three tachometers in one lock hold) would hold the
 lock for up to 4 EC reads or 36 PMC2 reads, longer than one uncached
 read today; the regulator's writes must not wait longer than before.
 Per input, a lock hold is either a cache hit (no I/O) or the same access
 as before. Upper bound on EC accesses from hwmon reads: 7 per second
 (each bounded by the 1 s budget), independent of the number of readers.
 .
 Errors (operator decision 2026-10-17): a refresh that ends in
 -ETIMEDOUT (a status wait ran out, the EC does not answer) is cached as
 that error for N5_CACHE_MS; reads in that window return -ETIMEDOUT from
 the cache without port I/O and without a new kernel warning. Without
 this, a reader flood while the EC hangs makes every read a refresh that
 holds the lock until its wait times out. -EAGAIN (torn tachometer read
 after four attempts; the EC answered) is not cached: the next read tries
 the EC again. The previous valid value is never served after a failed
 refresh because it is stale by then. The EC sentinels (temperature
 0x00/0xff, tachometer 0xffff) are successful EC answers: they are cached
 as raw values and still reported as -ENODATA (0007), also from the
 cache.
 .
 dead (0004/0006): checked before the cache, so after unbind/unload or
 .shutdown every read returns -ENODEV as before, cache or not. The cache
 lives in the devm-allocated n5_data and starts empty on every bind.
 .
 Lock: temperature and fan reads take data->lock with
 mutex_lock_killable(); a fatal signal while waiting returns -EINTR, no
 state is touched before the lock is held. Reason: these readers are
 unprivileged and may wait behind a refresh that uses up the 1 s budget;
 they can now be killed. The ecram reader (0008) uses the same. pwm and
 pwm_enable reads and all writes keep mutex_lock() and are unchanged.
 .
 Effect on the regulator: queued readers no longer add one EC access
 each. A pwm write waits for at most one refresh per input whose cache
 has expired (<= 7, each as long as one uncached read before) plus
 queued cache hits (no I/O). Before, it waited for one EC access per
 queued reader, without bound.
 .
 Residual risk: while the EC does not answer, each input still does one
 timed-out refresh per N5_CACHE_MS plus access time, independent of the
 number of readers; with all 7 inputs read in a loop the lock is busy
 for up to 7 timed-out accesses per such window. A reader that hits a
 cached -ETIMEDOUT (also n5-fangov) sees the error up to 1 s after the EC
 answers again. Fan RPM may be up to 1 s (plus the access time) old;
 n5-fangov reads at intervals of >= 2 s. debugfs dcr/ecram (root only)
 are not cached.
 .
 Checklist: C1/C2 unchanged (one EC transaction per lock hold), C3 (cache
 only under data->lock), C9 (lock hold per read <= before; cache hits
 without I/O), G5 unchanged (modes and visibility untouched), H5
 (MODULE_VERSION unchanged by this patch, 0008 sets 0.2.1+sr2~rc1).
 .
 Base: 0001-0007 (0.2.1+sr1~rc3; also applies on rc2) plus 0008. The
 0008 draft file was made on rc1 and does not apply to rc2/rc3 as is
 (header date hunk already in 0001, MODULE_VERSION context); its other
 hunks apply unchanged with offsets. Build test used that rebased 0008.
 .
 Tested: build/static analysis and userspace harness only
 (befunde/raw/p9-draft-build-20261016.txt; error caching:
 befunde/raw/rc3-build-20261017.txt, sections B/H).
 Not tested on hardware: values and timing of cached reads (sensors,
 n5-fangov), reader flood vs. pwm write latency, kill of a waiting reader,
 reads after unbind and after .shutdown.
